  "title": "EncounterService Save Encounter Impact",
  "updatedAt": 1793523067724,
  "answers": [
    {
      "id": "t8ijBiDWO48R9Vun",
      "productId": "openmrs",
      "question": "If the behaviour of `EncounterService.saveEncounter` in openmrs-core changed, which code in the REST web services module (openmrs-module-webservices.rest) uses it and could be affected?",
      "generation": "g-b59a21",
      "kind": "partial",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "## Declaration that could change\n`EncounterService.saveEncounter(Encounter)` is declared in the interface with a Spring Security `@PreAuthorize` on it, and implemented in `EncounterServiceImpl` (which does the permission checks, visit assignment, obs/order cascades and then `dao.saveEncounter`) [1] [2]. Its documented contract (cascade to obs, orders and encounter providers, encounter-condition saving, visit assignment, obs voiding/recreation, datetime/location propagation) is the behaviour a change would alter [3].",
          "citations": [
            1,
            2,
            3
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "## Confirmed affected code in webservices.rest (all in `omod`)",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "**1. `EncounterResource1_8.save` — the main REST entry point**\n- Returns `Context.getEncounterService().saveEncounter(enc)` directly, so every `POST /ws/rest/v1/encounter` (this resource covers OpenMRS \"1.8.*\") and every subsequent `POST /encounter/{uuid}` update goes through the changed method [4].\n- It is dispatched from the generic CRUD layer: `DelegatingCrudResource.create` calls `save(delegate)` after converting the posted body [5] and `DelegatingCrudResource.update` calls it again after applying the updatable properties [6]. So the request body is built from `getCreatableProperties()`/`getUpdatableProperties()` — encounterDatetime, patient, encounterType, location, form, provider, orders, obs [7]; each cascade the service performs (obs, orders, encounter providers, conditions, visits) is therefore observable through this endpoint.\n- `newDelegate()` pre-seeds `encounterDatetime = now()` and a non-null orders set, which feeds into the service's \"is new encounter\" branching [8].",
          "citations": [
            4,
            5,
            6,
            7,
            8
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**2. `EncounterResource1_9.save` — overrides the above for OpenMRS 1.9–2.1**\n- It first re-points each `EncounterProvider` at the encounter (a workaround because providers were otherwise persisted with a null `encounter_id`), then calls `saveEncounter(delegate)` and returns the same object rather than the service's return value [9]. This subclass is the registered `/encounter` resource for 1.9.*–2.1.* and adds `visit` and `encounterProviders` to the creatable/updatable properties [10] [11]. Note the returned object is not the persisted instance, so any behaviour where the service returns a different/refreshed Encounter (e.g. a re-fetch inside the service) would show up here as a stale response body.",
          "citations": [
            9,
            10,
            11
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**3. `EncounterProviderResource1_9` — two calls, on the sub-resource**\n- `save(EncounterProvider)` calls `addProvider(...)` on the parent encounter and then `saveEncounter(delegate.getEncounter())`, then hunts the provider back out of `getEncounterProviders()` to return the persisted uuid — it explicitly relies on `addProvider` not returning the created provider [12].\n- `delete(...)` is a workaround for TRUNK-5017: it marks matching `EncounterProvider`s voided in memory and persists the whole parent encounter via `saveEncounter` omod/src/main/java/org/openmrs/module/webservices/rest/web/v1_0/resource/openmrs1_9/EncounterProviderResource1_9.java.\n- This matters because `EncounterServiceImpl.saveEncounter` cascades encounter-provider save/delete and the `EncounterResource1_9.save` workaround exists precisely for provider persistence api/src/main/java/org/openmrs/api/EncounterService.java; a change in either cascade would break these \"hack\" paths and their assertions.",
          "citations": [
            12
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "### How a request reaches it\n```mermaid\n---\ntitle: REST encounter save path\n---\nflowchart TD\n  post[\"POST /ws/rest/v1/encounter\"] --> crud[\"DelegatingCrudResource.create\"]\n  update[\"POST /encounter/{uuid}\"] --> crudUpdate[\"DelegatingCrudResource.update\"]\n  crud --> res18[\"EncounterResource1_8.save\"]\n  crudUpdate --> res18\n  res18 --> res19[\"EncounterResource1_9.save override\"]\n  res19 --> svc[\"EncounterService.saveEncounter\"]\n  provider[\"EncounterProviderResource1_9 save/delete\"] --> svc\n  svc --> impl[\"EncounterServiceImpl\"]\n```",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "## Test code that also calls it (would need updating / could fail)\n- `EncounterController1_9Test` calls `saveEncounter(enc)` to persist a voided encounter before asserting `GET /encounter?patient=...` filtering, twice: omod/src/test/java/org/openmrs/module/webservices/rest/web/v1_0/controller/openmrs1_9/EncounterController1_9Test.java and omod/src/test/java/org/openmrs/module/webservices/rest/web/v1_0/controller/openmrs1_9/EncounterController1_9Test.java. These assert a count of 2 encounters, so they depend on save's voiding/datetime behaviour.\n- `OrderController1_10Test.shouldReviseAnActiveOrder` builds an `Encounter` and saves it via `es.saveEncounter(encounter)` so it can attach the revised order to it omod/src/test/java/org/openmrs/module/webservices/rest/web/v1_0/controller/openmrs1_10/OrderController1_10Test.java.",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "## Likely but unverified / out of scope\n- Indirect REST reads: `EncounterResource1_8` search paths use `getEncountersByPatient` / `ServiceSearcher` omod/src/main/java/org/openmrs/module/webservices/rest/web/v1_0/resource/openmrs1_8/EncounterResource1_8.java and `EncounterSearchHandler2_0` uses `getEncounters(criteria)` omod/src/main/java/org/openmrs/module/webservices/rest/web/v1_0/search/openmrs2_0/EncounterSearchHandler2_0.java; these don't call `saveEncounter` but the assertions in `EncounterController1_9Test` pair them with a save, so they only break indirectly.\n- The `EncounterService` name appears in other resources for unrelated methods (`saveEncounterType`, `saveEncounterRole`, `getEncounterByUuid`, `getEncounterRolesByName`) plus `VisitConfigurationController2_0` for the \"assign to visit\" handler setting omod/src/main/java/org/openmrs/module/webservices/rest/web/v1_0/controller/openmrs2_0/VisitConfigurationController2_0.java. These are name clashes, not callers of `saveEncounter`, but a change to visit assignment inside `saveEncounter` would interact with that handler.\n- The JS/frontend consumers (esm-patient-chart and others in scope) never call the Java API; they hit the REST paths above, so they are affected only through the JSON contract of `/encounter`.\n- Not checked: other repositories that call `EncounterService.saveEncounter` outside webservices.rest, dynamic/`Context`-based lookups, and reflection-based resource registration.",
          "citations": []
        }
      ],
